"The Ring", "Polygon", "Thomas", "Rose", "Los Angeles", "Butler", "America", "Disney Plus", "Hulu", "Freevee", "Henry", "Greek mythology", "AIDS", "London", "TBS", "Squid Game", "Betty", "The Exorcist", "William Peter Blatty", "Max", "Paramount Plus", "horror", "Criterion Channel", "Pete Volk", "Sam Raimi", "Kanopy", "Austen Goslin", "horror movies", "body horror", "Cure", "Kiyoshi Kurosawa", "Eyes Without a Face", "Georges Franju", "Prime Video", "Shudder", "Hoopla", "Song Kang-ho", "John Carpenter", "love", "Matt Patches", "Stephen King", "George A. Romero", "Peacock", "zombies", "AMC Plus", "Dario Argento", "The Texas Chain Saw Massacre", "Tobe Hooper", "Olivia Rodrigo", "World War II", "Nazis", "Ring", "Lovecraft", "Josh Ruben", "Hot Topic", "Petrana Radulovic", "internet", "Tech companies", "What to Watch"], "page": "The Halloween Countdown: 31 days of horror to watch (Polygon, 2023-10-10T15:27:53+00:00)", "passage": "The Halloween Countdown: 31 days of horror to watch (Polygon, 2023-10-10T15:27:53+00:00)\nProduced after Kurosawa’s Cure (which we featured on last year’s Halloween calendar), Pulse is widely celebrated as one of the foundational texts in the canon of J-horror cinema, alongside Hideo Nakata’s Ring and Takashi Shimizu’s Ju-on: The Curse. Eerie and methodical, Kurosawa’s film is a nihilistic meditation on technology and human relationships that presages an advent of loneliness in a world growing more and more “connected” with each passing day. Rife with imagery that will stay with you long after it’s over, Pulse is a tremendous and terrifying movie worth experiencing. —TE\n\nOct. 5: Apostle\n\nWhere to watch: Netflix\n\nDon’t confuse Gareth Edwards (director of The Creator and Rogue One: A Star Wars Story) with Gareth Evans, director of the Raid movies. If you do, you may sit down to the Netflix-exclusive Apostle expecting expansive, detail-driven science fiction, and be unpleasantly surprised when you get a grimy, extremely gory period piece that g